[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The failure is caused by the routing control-plane on leaf_router_0_0: its FRR BGP daemon (bgpd) is not running. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1, pings receive “Destination Net Unreachable” from 10.0.0.1 (leaf_router_0_0), and in the reverse direction from 10.0.1.1 (leaf_router_0_1). On leaf_router_0_0, the IP route table contains only connected routes (no BGP-learned prefixes), and frr_get_bgp_conf reports “bgpd is not running.” Meanwhile, the physical fabric links are up: leaf_router_0_0’s uplinks eth0 (172.16.0.5/31) and eth1 (172.16.0.9/31) are UP/LOWER_UP, disproving a cabling fault. Upstream, the spines and super-spine have a healthy BGP view of 10.0.1.0/24 from leaf_router_0_1, but there is no 10.0.0.0/24 because leaf_router_0_0 is not advertising it. Mechanism: with bgpd down on leaf_router_0_0, it neither learns 10.0.1.0/24 nor advertises 10.0.0.0/24, so it returns ICMP net-unreachable to its host and the opposite leaf does the same for return traffic. Localization: leaf_router_0_0, process bgpd; interfaces eth0/eth1 are fine. Root cause: bgpd on leaf_router_0_0 stopped/crashed. Corrective fix: restart the BGP service on leaf_router_0_0 (preferably restart the FRR/bgpd service), then verify BGP neighbors and that both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 reappear in the fabric routing tables. No cable reseat is required.
=== END ===